# Children of the Five Winds — The Fox Clan

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## The Blessings of the Sands

Sofu leaned forward and puckered his lips with exaggerated kissing noises, sounding not unlike a fish gasping for air. The other villagers laughed, and Sofu frowned. "I'm serious. She was beautiful! And when I reached out to propose marriage, she slipped from my grasp and melted into the form of a fox!"

That detail was too much—not the fox, but the proposal. Cries of "No!" and "Why you?" and "Not with the sloppy kiss sounds!"

"Sofu, what quality do you have that a kitsune would want?" someone shouted.

"I have plenty of good qualities—"The laughter turned him red, and the crowd parted ways, returning to their daily chores.

He stomped his feet in the dirt, gritting his teeth, but in the background he saw a flash of crimson-orange.

"Wait, wait, wait! Come back! Fine, fine, fine. Let me tell you the truth about that night."

The villagers all turned back to Sofu, unaware of the foxes stealing food from the merchants' carts. Sofu hid his grin and spun his tale…

## The Fox Clan

Humankind is fond of the notion that they are separate from nature, dividing the world between "civilization" and the untamed wilderness. But this is not so simple, least of all in the Emerald Empire, where spirits dwell and Fortunes walk among mortals. Ningen-dō, the Mortal Realm, overlaps with Senkyō, the "enchanted country" of animals and tricksters. Many of Senkyō's denizens live side by side with humans, often hidden in plain sight. Some grow so old and powerful as to be worshipped as gods. And while some are benevolent, others are not so kind. Humans may have mastered their environment, but they cannot master nature itself.

From their home in the Kitsune Forest, the rustic shugenja of the tiny Fox Minor Clan commune with the spirits of Senkyō. By forging treaties with shapeshifting yōkai, the Fox have not only survived but thrived within the haunted woods, erecting a small castle and enjoying the bounty of the farmlands nearby. But their settlements exist in the liminal space between Ningen-dō and Senkyō, a place that has long rejected human presence. Enduring incredible hardship, pushed to the brink of extinction, the Fox Clan would have faded out long ago had it not been for the benevolence of their guardians, supernatural foxes called kitsune.

Most of the clan's shugenja are partially descended from these fox spirits, their ancestors having secured their alliance by intermarriage with their supernatural guardians. This yōkai lineage grants Fox shugenja subtle powers, including the gift (or what some might call the curse) of shapeshifting. Thanks to the blood of the fox, the Kitsune Forest recognizes them as kin. Together, the Fox Clan helps to maintain the harmony of their forest home, serving the benevolent spirits that accepted them and appeasing the tricksters who will not.

Fox shugenja's influence with nature spirits often extends beyond the forest bounds. These shugenja are famous for their affinity with animals. As representatives of Senkyō and advocates for the natural world, they come to the defense of endangered forests, even arguing before the Emperor against deforestation and the exploitation of animal life. And when lawful pleas fail to halt an ambitious lord's expansion, the Fox get "creative." For they do not truly feel that they are a part of the same empire as the other clans. Their world is the forest now. They will do what they must to protect it.

> **FOX SPIRITS**
>
> Kitsune are fox spirits from Chikushō-dō, the Realm of Animals, which is part of Senkyō. Many transformed from a mundane fox to a kitsune after reaching a certain age. They commonly have unusual markings or two or more tails, and they are sometimes accompanied by floating balls of flame known as kitsune-bi. As shapechanging tricksters, foxes, especially those known as yako, or "field foxes," love to pull pranks on the unsuspecting. Some become fascinated with human beings, adopting human form in order to live among them. Such disguises are imperfect, and those who know what to look for can still identify them.

> **ANIMAL SPIRITS AND YŌKAI**
>
> Most animals and animal spirits conceptualize the Celestial Order as follows: demons ascend to become animals, animals ascend to become yōkai (supernatural beings) or animal spirits (like tengu, kitsu, kitsune, and so forth), yōkai and animal spirits ascend to become humans, and humans ascend to become spirits or Fortunes.

### THE FOUNDING

When the Ki-Rin Clan left the Empire in the first century, a remnant remained behind to safeguard Shinjo's lands in her absence. Known as the Kaichi, they became Shinjo's voice in the courts, carrying her word on her behalf. Recording historic events for Shinjo's return from what would one day become Battle Maiden Castle and erecting a shrine to Inari, the Fortune of Rice, the Ki-Rin stewards enjoyed great prosperity for nearly two generations.

But many in the other clans resented them. They were a group that, with no true daimyō or granted family name, nonetheless claimed vast tracts of land and spoke with a Kami's authority. So it was that, when the Seppun declared the Ki-Rin lost, the Kaichi experienced a rapid decline in the perceived weight of their words.

Soon after, a Yobanjin force invaded Rokugan from the western border. The Kaichi were swept aside, and the Ki-Rin lands would have been completely lost had it not been for the timely intervention of the Lion Clan. But after the Lion drove the invaders away, they refused to return the Ki-Rin lands. After all, the former inhabitants had proven to be inadequate defenders, and wasn't the security of the border more important than restoring the remnants of a lost clan? Emperor Hantei Genji had the safety of his father's empire to consider, and the remnants of the Ki-Rin became permanent refugees in the care of the Phoenix Clan.

Then came the Lion-Phoenix War. Their Shiba guardians were unable to protect the Kaichi; the Lion slaughtered most of the remaining Ki-Rin. The treaties

that ended the war granted the Lion full custody of the ancestral Ki-Rin lands and relocated the surviving Ki-Rin to an uninhabited forest south of the Spine of the World Mountains. The Emperor declared these remnants a clan and named them after the forest to which they were relocated. Thus, they became the Kitsune Minor Clan, commonly called the Fox Clan, adopting the fox as their symbol. Isolated and facing starvation, the survivors were forced to abandon their prior ways. Within a generation, they had resorted to laboring, game hunting, and banditry just to survive. Although considered a clan, they lived like mere rōnin, shades of what they had once been. So it would have remained, had it not been for the unusual nature of their new home. For in truth, the Emperor had no authority to grant those lands to anyone—they already belonged to the primordial nature spirits of Senkyō, denizens who were becoming curious about the strange human neighbors who now shared their name: kitsune. The forest was a part of the Enchanted Country. To survive, the Fox Clan would have to bow to its power.

### THE CHILDREN OF SHINUN

Years later, an unassuming hunter named Shinun discovered two foxes in her hare traps. She knew their pelts would fetch a good price, and the Fox Clan was always starving. But one of the foxes was pregnant, and she was loath to harm either of them. While she considered what to do, the foxes suddenly spoke to her. They promised to lead her to a splendid golden land rich with food if she released them. Shinun did, even taking the time to bandage and treat the male's wounded paw. The foxes led her through many eerie bamboo groves, through clefts in the earth and beneath roots. Eventually, she found herself in a shadowy copse of enoki trees, cast in the verdant light of floating orbs of fox fire. She knew she was in Chikushō-dō, the blessed Realm of Animals. At the foot of a giant fox statue sat more food than she could eat in a year, from simple rice and steamed buns to sweet pastries and roasted meats. Two jugs stood by the statue's sides: from one flowed an endless stream of sake—from the other, endless water. The foxes offered her as much of the bounty as she desired. Shinun stuffed her bags with as much as she could to feed her people, choosing filling grains and vegetables over meat and sweets. Rather than claim the jugs, she merely filled her gourd with water. After bowing to the statue, she asked to be returned home.

This intrigued the foxes; they had assumed she would choose the delicacies, drink sake until drunk, or abandon her people to dwell in luxury forever. Shinun never saw how the untouched sweets transformed into stinging insects or how the meat and sake soured into poison. Unwittingly, she had avoided the grove's trap. The foxes decided she was worth watching. A season passed before Shinun saw them again. This time they approached in human form: a man and a woman carrying a litter of kits. Revealing their identities, they asked Shinun to name each kit. When she did, they became partially human, and the two fox spirits jointly proposed to her. If she helped raise their kits, the two fox spirits would become Shinun's spouses, helping her form a bloodline that the forest would recognize, one connected by fox blood to Senkyō itself. This was the start of the Kitsune family line. With time, the forest would recognize her clan as kin, embracing its members as its own. This is how Kitsune Shinun made an ancestral pact with yōkai, becoming the first Fox Clan shugenja.

### THE LANDS OF THE FOX CLAN

While legends of the Kitsune Forest are widespread, few visitors travel to the lands of the Fox Clan. They are tucked away in an isolated part of the Empire where there is little of note. Yet the Fox frequently host diplomatic envoys from the Great Clans, and the Winter Courts of Kyūden Kitsune are somewhat infamous for supernatural occurrences and impromptu dalliances, as though the forest itself influences the proceedings. Still, the Fox are wary of outsiders, if only because unexpected humans may upset the delicate balance of power or compromise their spiritual treaties. The Fox's outermost territories, few in number, consist of stacked farmlands fed by the flooding of the nearby River of Gold. Across this river, the vibrant Kitsune Forest rises in contrast to the Uebe Marshes of the Crane Clan. This ancient wood was once a part of the primordial Shinomen Forest before time and shifting land separated them. The Kitsune Forest is a dreamlike beauty, teeming with life and yet still as a painting. Lush moss carpets the forest floor, while the thick canopy filters light and reduces harsh storms to gentle rains. Hidden copses and pathways lie scattered through the wood, and shimmering cascades flow through the roots of massive oaks. The emerald glow is broken by white flowers, purple-blooming wisterias, and splashes of orange maple. The entire forest seems taken by whimsy, tempting visitors to lower their guard.

> **THE KITSUNE FOREST**
>
> Read more about the Kitsune Forest in Emerald Empire: The Essential Guide to Rokugan, pages 210–211.

Travelers are wise to camp on the road. To do otherwise is to tempt the forest spirits, who are often drawn to the unwary and take offense easily. Strangers crossing paths on this road simply disregard one another, not even stopping to bow. After all, one can never tell whether such a stranger is really a mischievous trickster in disguise. Victims of a spirit's prank should take it in stride, as spirits tend to bestow favor on those who do.

When the Kitsune family chose to build their home at the fork of the River of Gold, sourcing the wood from other forests and without disturbing the area's natural features, it was out of respect for the Kitsune Forest on the other side of the river. They believed that the forest yōkai would reject any human-made dwellings within it. But in the following centuries, the forest has reached across the river to embrace the keep, and now the palace is surrounded by lush woods. Although the keep is only an hour's hike into the forest, the path is obscured, and it is easy to get lost.

Visitors expecting a backwater folksy community are taken aback upon arriving at Kyūden Kitsune. The Castle of the Fox is every bit the distinguished palace, offering all the expected amenities of a true kyūden. This traditional, seven-story castle has even been the site of several Imperial Winter Courts, offering an oasis of urban comforts in the rustic wilderness. The surrounding town, while modest, is likewise similar to any other forest community, except perhaps for the pointed lack of loggers. Bushi often remark that

> **KITSUNEHIME**
>
> Several tapestries within the Castle of the Fox depict an Otherwordly being woman with long white hair, two large fox ears, and ten white fox tails. Clad in a spider-silk kimono, she holds a lotus blossom in one hand and is surrounded by orbs of emerald flame. This is Kitsunehime, an ancient guardian of the Fox Clan who is said to watch over the groves that are sacred to Inari. Other tales attest that she is a servant of Kisshōten, the Fortune of Happiness and Fertility, helping the forest to thrive. Still others say she was a fox that grew old enough to sprout nine tails but, instead of becoming a god, chose to grow a tenth tail and remain in the forest. She occasionally visits the dreams of Fox Clan heroes to warn of coming danger.

both the castle and its town are not very defensible, and yet those who live here do not seem particularly concerned about this.

Within the palace grounds is Shinun's Orchard, an impressive copse of persimmon and akebia trees surrounded by ancient oaks. Blessed shimenawa ropes hug the most ancient trees, and ivy embraces its small shrine to Inari. Though the orchard produces fruit throughout the year, some may wonder if these "gardens" are not simply patches of woodland left to their own devices.

Of course, the mischievous Kitsune Forest teems just beyond the castle walls, and the palace doors are open to all visitors, human and otherwise. Guests are gently steered away from chambers that are seemingly abandoned. Some have reported waking in a different room than the one where they fell asleep. Others claim they were invited to tea ceremonies or moon viewings by unfamiliar nobles, only to suddenly find themselves in a bamboo grove, facing a long walk back to the palace in the dark.

### Autumn Oak Library

Visitors to Kyūden Kitsune sometimes mistake the stone complex suspended over the River of Gold as an extension of the palace or a massive shrine. In truth, the Autumn Oak Library is one of the oldest lore depositories in the Empire. It was built on columns to raise the archives from the ground and protect the texts within from humidity and the elements, while the wooden fixtures are noxious to termites and other insects.

It is customary for long-staying guests to leave something behind for this library: a tome, a scroll, or some other curiosity. This has led to the library's steady growth over the years, although it is not nearly as extensive as the libraries of the Great Clans. Its greatest treasure is a collection of firsthand accounts dating from the dawn of the Empire, histories originally intended for the children of Shinjo. Other clans sometimes request access to the library's genealogical and historical information, which predates the first Lion-Phoenix War and includes several firsthand accounts of ancient battles against wicked forces.

### BENDING TO THE WOODS

The Fox Clan's way of life is a balance between the civilized dignity of a samurai and the realities of isolation within an enchanted forest. Most live a lifestyle similar to that of a typical samurai. But the Fox also know that they dwell here only at the convenience of the inscrutable lords of Senkyō. Their way of life is therefore inevitably connected to the Kitsune Forest, and it occasionally requires them to compromise their station. For instance, while most samurai consider manual labor to be beneath them, Fox samurai participate in extensive forestry projects, managing the forest's upkeep by clearing away dead brush, preventing deer migration trails from becoming too overgrown, maintaining the clarity of forest streams, and so on. They mark kodama trees—home to tree spirits—restore depleted soil, and leave offerings in hidden sacred places. In exchange, the guardians of the woods bestow their favor on the tiny clan.

Additionally, the clan's cuisine is far more relaxed than is typical: it includes game animals, and Fox may eat communally from the same dish. Yet there are certain foods they can never consume and parts of the forest that are permanently off-limits. Outsiders sometimes balk at the strange rules the Kitsune family enforces while beneath forest boughs. Certain words can never be uttered. Certain rituals must always be performed. When encountering a fox, one must always leave an offering, even if it means sacrificing the last of one's rations. They may seem like superstitions, but the Fox cannot guarantee the safety of a guest who disregards them. The forest has a way of humbling the prideful, and the Fox know better than to get in its way.

One way the Fox maintain their bond with their guardians is through intermarriage. Kitsune can take human form, and their offspring inherit a few of their otherworldly abilities. These individuals often become powerful shugenja or exceptionally charming courtiers. They also must reckon with the otherworldly aspects they have inherited: involuntary transformations are

common, especially in the light of the full moon, when losing control of one's emotions, or during certain seasons. They are also more susceptible to sudden possession by fox spirits. While these things are without stigma within the clan (and indeed seen as a sign of favor when they occur), the world beyond the forest is considerably less understanding. Only a fraction of Fox Clan members have the blood of foxes in their lineage, but this is not the true source of a Fox shugenja's abilities. While members of other shugenja families must be born with a natural rapport with the kami, a Fox shugenja has made a pact with a yōkai, securing their allegiance in exchange for offerings, reverence, and other favors. This otherworldly being guardian can command the elements on the shugenja's behalf, perform supernatural favors, grant the shugenja access to the abilities connected to their animal self, or even teach the shugenja elemental rituals and secrets. However, over the generations of Fox Clan shugenja, undeniable ties to certain spirits have formed. Usually the eldest child in the family inherits one of the family's guardian spirits when one of their elders passes. When there are many children or a child's temperament does not align with that of an available familial animal spirit, this is when a child would need to find their own spirit in the wilds. When a Fox shugenja comes of age, their first task is to enter the woods alone and seek an animal spirit if none of the familial guardian spirits are suitable or available. Most often they find a kitsune, but they may find any of the other forest yōkai, such as a river otter (kawauso), raccoon dog (tanuki), or forest cat (bakeneko). Especially bold or ambitious shugenja may seek more ancient beings. Once an audience is secured, the shugenja must make a deal with the spirit. The agreement binds them together; subsequently, both grow stronger and take on aspects of the other. From that point on, the animal spirit is always nearby, watching over and protecting the shugenja, sometimes even manifesting as a companion or posing as a pet. Often the two become mutual confidants, even lifelong friends, as few know the true heart of a Fox shugenja like their guardian yōkai. This practice would be a source of superstition and mistrust elsewhere; most samurai would never make a deal with a yōkai. Spirits are unpredictable, and some demands might be considered taboo. The most strict-minded samurai draw comparisons between such pacts and blood magic. The Fox have no such qualms. Their children play in the woods alongside spirits and animals alike (with supervision), and shapeshifting tricksters come and go as they please. A Fox samurai might start their day training in the dōjō of Kyūden Kitsune and end it performing an inscrutable task in the deep woods

> **GUARDIAN YŌKAI AND TSUKEMONO-SUJI**
>
> The practice of gaining guardian yōkai and animal familiars exists elsewhere in the Empire. Witches known as tsukemono-suji possess magical familiars that are passed down through bloodlines.

> **THE REALMS OF SENKYŌ**
>
> Senkyō is divided into two overlapping realms. Chikushō-dō is the realm of animal spirits. These spirits seek to coexist with civilization and be reborn into better forms. Sakkaku is the realm of tricksters and mischief; its denizens delight in pranks and malicious trickery. An animal yōkai might hail from either realm, and it is not always immediately obvious to which a spirit may belong. A Fox Clan shugenja makes deals with either or both of these "courts." Some Fox shugenja exclusively commune with the benevolent denizens of Chikushō-dō, becoming spiritual counselors for animals and forest guardians. Others forge pacts with the tricksters of Sakkaku, often becoming tricksters themselves. Fox Clan culture does not favor one over the other. One does what is necessary to preserve the natural harmony. One does what is necessary to survive.

for a raccoon dog. The Fox make compromises other samurai would not; personal integrity and clout always seem to come second to basic survival. But then, survival is what the Fox do best. They have endured generations of hardship and grown from it. They embrace challenge and are willing to do what it takes to survive. Their outlook is summarized by a colloquial mantra: "Fall down seven times, stand up eight."

The Fox remain estranged from the rest of the Empire. They learned the hard way what it means to become entangled in Great Clan affairs. As such, the Fox have frequent difficulty securing clan allies. This is most often because they are associated with trickster spirits, and many consider it unlucky to have dealings with "fox witches." The Fox are known to leverage these superstitions to their own advantage, which does not help things. Many feel that the Fox cannot be trusted, as they win their battles with trickery and supernatural favors. Still, while the Fox prefer to keep the rest of the Empire at arm's length, they never fully withdraw. They have a representative in nearly every major court, and whenever Senkyō needs a voice, a Fox is there to give it. Their priests are sought for counsel on nature-related

matters, especially due to their reputation as "animal whisperers." For instance, a lord whose domain seems plagued by devious pranks and evidence of mischief might seek the Fox Clan's counsel before taking matters into their own hands. Further, Fox shugenja are regarded as the Empire's foremost carers of animals, and they are entrusted with the care of beloved pets as regularly as they are with beasts of burden. Of course, many a Fox Clan samurai has found themself sympathizing with the plight of animals whose environment has been encroached upon by the steady march of civilization. So even though the Fox prefer to remain separate observers, the reality is not so simple.

The Fox's greatest rivalry is with the Hare Clan, an aggressive Minor Clan that has expanded several times in its history. The Hare and Fox came to blows merely a generation ago, a conflict that ended only due to the intervention of the Crane. The Fox also had a brief logging disagreement with the Sparrow Clan, a misunderstanding that the Suzume family is still trying to repair.

The Fox Clan's relationships with most Great Clans are strained. In the ninth century, the Crab Champion's son suggested the Fox be forcibly reabsorbed into the Unicorn, and the Fox have never forgotten the insult. They sometimes stir up trouble with the Crab over deforestation and mining. Similarly, the Fox have never forgiven the Lion for the suffering and disgrace they inflicted on their ancestors. Even though they owe their very identity to this event, they cannot forget the numerous injustices, and they see the Lion as opportunistic hypocrites.

The Fox do have some uneasy allies. Their alliance with the Crane was recently strengthened by the marriage of Kitsune Kuzunobu, the Fox daimyō's great-nephew, to Doji Hotaru, the Crane Clan Champion. The Scorpion also keep close ties with the Fox, and they are united with them through marriage with the Soshi. After the Unicorn's return, the Fox invited the Scorpion to visit the Autumn Oak Library to share knowledge. The two clans have enjoyed an amiable relationship ever since, and the Unicorn sometimes refer to them as cousins. Yet there will always be a rift between them, and their lack of proximity means they rarely interact.

> **INHERITED ENEMIES**
>
> The Kitsune family took its name from both the Kitsune Forest and the shapeshifting foxes that became its protectors. There is great power in names, but there are also unintended consequences.
> Long ago, the shapeshifting spider yōkai known as jorōgumo swore an unending blood feud with "any who are kitsune." Thus, the Kitsune family has also inherited this curse. Several of the family's superstitions are preoccupied with avoiding the jorōgumo's attention, and while most garden and house spiders are considered harmless, orb-weaver spiders are considered extremely unlucky and are to be swept from the garden.

The Phoenix are often aligned with the Fox, but a theological and fundamental disagreement regarding the Bushidō tenet of Righteousness sometimes drives a wedge between them. The Phoenix believe harmony is a natural state resulting from justice, but the Fox have seen too much of the forest's uncaring savagery to believe that justice occurs in nature. To them, justice is a mortal invention, and peace does not always mean true harmony has been accomplished. The two clans debate this point often and publicly.

> **KITSUNE RYŌSEI**
>
> This profile is for the real Kitsune Ryōsei, who may be wandering the Empire. She is incognito, but her green eyes, lack of worldliness, or keepsakes from home may give her away. She is running from her responsibilities, and the PCs may feel compelled to convince her to return home. Or perhaps they will be willing to accompany her on a few adventures. She is, after all, very persistent.
> To represent the trickster spirit that is impersonating her, use this same profile, but apply the Trickster Animal in Disguise template, page 140.

### ADVENTURE SEED: HUNTING NOT PERMITTED

- Hook — While participating in or observing a hunting competition sponsored by a local governor, the PCs learn of a lone shugenja sabotaging the event from the safety of the woods. Eventually, the shugenja proves too much of a nuisance, and the PCs are asked to find and appease him or otherwise end the trouble so the hunting can proceed.
- Rising Action — The shugenja proves difficult to track. Either the PCs manage to trap him or he ambushes them directly. Hailing from the Fox Clan, the shugenja claims to be trying to save the nearby town. If allowed to explain, he reveals that the woods are the realm of a primordial Forest Lord and that if any animal blood is shed there by human hands, the supernatural entity will retaliate. But the shugenja refuses to meet with the governor and flees if it is suggested.
- Climax — If the hunting party cannot be convinced to stop, it will inevitably incur the wrath of the Forest Lord, which will harm not only the governor but also the innocent townsfolk. However, it is too late to stop the competition, as the overzealous competitors seek glory for their clans. The shugenja is afraid to approach the governor directly because he is actually the governor's illegitimate son by a kitsune spirit, and he will lose his freedom (and scandalize his father) if his origin is revealed. If the PCs can somehow prevent loss of life without disgracing any of the involved parties, they will receive the gratitude of everyone involved, including the Forest Lords of Senkyō.
Ryōsei can talk to animals and understands their speech.

While in dense or forested surroundings, Ryōsei ignores the effects of Entangling and Obscuring terrain. Once per scene as a Support action, she may extend this ability to up to two targets at range 0–1. The effects of this ability last for as long as affected targets remain in these surroundings.

When Ryōsei performs an action to activate an invocation, she may spend 焔 in the following way:

Additionally, she knows the following invocations: Path of Beasts (Celestial Realms, page 109), Messenger of Chikushō-dō (Celestial Realms, page 106), Nature's Touch (core rulebook, page 194), and Inari's Blessing (core rulebook, page 209).

Players who wish to build a Fox Clan character may do so using the clan and family information provided in this section. At the GM's discretion, a Fox character could study at a Great Clan school, especially if this was arranged as a favor or if the character is not a shugenja. Conversely, a character from one of the Great Clans might study at a Fox Clan school if they have an affinity with nature or an animal yōkai in their bloodline. The rules for the Kitsune Naturalist School and the Kitsune Mediator School can be found on pages 87 and 86. Additional Fox Clan character options are suggested in the accompanying sidebar.

> **WHAT DOES YOUR CHARACTER KNOW?**
>
> All Fox Clan characters have greater awareness of the following topics:
> - You have a strong grasp of the political situation and traditions of your clan, the Kitsune Forest, and the surrounding lands. This may include knowledge of the various treaties and pacts your clan has made with animal lords of the forest. You also know much about your clan's history and the early history of the Ki-Rin Clan.
> - You have considerable knowledge about the Kitsune Forest, animal yōkai, and animal behavior. You know how to navigate encounters with animals, and you know the details of many superstitions pertaining to shapechanging animal spirits prone to trickery. You may even know one or two hidden ways to enter Senkyō, the "enchanted country."
> - You know how to survive far from civilization. You can find your way through the woods, identify plants and herbs, and sleep comfortably pretty much anywhere—even on a castle rooftop or in a lord's garden.
> - You probably have firsthand experience with shapeshifting tricksters and talking animals. There may even be one in your immediate family!

> **STUDENT OF THE TALON**
>
> Many Fox Clan samurai train as a hunter, guard, or other bushi in order to protect their clan. Players building a Fox Clan bushi character may wish to consider the Student of the Talon School in Path of Waves: The Essential Guide to Rōnin, page 49.
> For Fox Clan characters with animal spirits in their bloodline, players might consider the Kitsune Impersonator Tradition in Emerald Empire: The Essential Guide to Rokugan, page 239, the Bond with a Spirit title in Celestial Realms, page 140, or both.

> **NEW TECHNIQUES INTRODUCED IN THIS BOOK**
>
> Most of the techniques listed in the school profiles described on these and the following pages are covered in the core rulebook in Chapter 4, starting on page 171. Those shown in bold here, such as Thunderbolt Style, are new techniques not found in the core rulebook.
