### The Fox Minor Clan

### The Kitsune Family

> **WHAT DOES BUSHIDŌ MEAN TO YOUR CLAN?**
>
> The Fox see Bushidō as a warrior's ideal, and since spirits tend to favor virtuous individuals, they consider it a useful moral guide. Yet the Fox are skeptical of those who exalt it. It is their experience that, when a virtue is inconvenient, it is disregarded. They need look no further than the injustices they suffered at the hands of the Lion, so-called paragons of these virtues, to justify this opinion. They also know all too well that nature does not follow any mortal virtues; only survival matters to the forest and its denizens. The reality of their situation means they do not have the luxury of following Bushidō too closely.
> Perhaps this, and their hesitancy to trust others, explains why they value the tenets of Righteousness and Compassion so highly. These virtues make life easier in the unforgiving wilderness; members of the Fox are often torn between the urge to right a witnessed wrong and the desire to remain uninvolved. As a clan that relies on trickery to survive, and as a people who hide their true selves, Sincerity is the tenet they most struggle with.
> See page 301 of the core rulebook for more information on how these can affect your honor and glory.

### New Schools

The following are new schools that PCs can select as part of character creation.

#### Ganzu Guardian Tradition [Bushi]

The Ganzu people who reside in the Hidden Valley are in some ways the sixth great family of the Unicorn Clan, despite being little known within Rokugan's bounds. Their lands occupy a key oasis along the Sand Road, making the Ganzu a crucial vassal of the Unicorn, and their warriors often serve as part of Unicorn cohorts, especially outside of Rokugan. Given the value of their oasis, they have long had to protect it from outside powers. Their battle traditions are suited to the terrain where they most often fight: the searing deserts near their homeland, where the heat can be more lethal than the enemy, and a lack of preparation deadlier than a sword. As such, the Ganzu teach their warriors to fight with precision, wasting little effort and letting the foe exhaust themself before striking the decisive blow. Their martial styles favor a variety of weapons, but many Ganzu warriors specialize in the ax or the sword.

- Kata: ✦ Slashing Sandstorm Style
- Shūji (choose one): Honest Assessment, Shallow Waters
Moment of Certainty (School Ability): Once per scene after you defend against damage, you may roll a number of skill dice equal to the fatigue you received this way. Choose a number of those dice up to your school rank and reserve them. Until the end of the scene, when you perform an Attack action check, after choosing kept dice, you may add one of the dice you reserved this way as an additional kept die.

Starting Outfit: Traveling clothes, concealed armor, Ganzu ring ax, two knives, traveling rations (eight days).

### Ide Emissary School [Courtier, Bushi]

The Ide family of the Unicorn Clan specializes in retaining relationships through trade and good will. And while the family's traders amass fortunes in foreign markets, the family's emissaries use that wealth to further the interests of the Unicorn Clan. Ide Emissaries are expected to learn the customs, protocols, and styles of any court they visit, whether in the castle of a Rokugani lord, in the ordu of an influential Ujik leader, or in one of the spires of academia in the Qamarist Caliphate. Not only must they find common ground with people of widely different cultures, but they must insinuate themselves into courts where power dynamics and alliances have existed for centuries or millennia, and each group looks at any new player as both a risk and an opportunity. This requires a great deal of education; most Ide Emissaries study alongside mentors who have served in far-flung lands for years before being chosen for a major assignment.

Rings: +1 Fire, +1 Water

Starting Skills (choose five): +1 Courtesy, +1 Culture, +1 Games, +1 Government, +1 Martial Arts [Unarmed], +1 Sentiment, +1 Survival

Honor: 50

Techniques Available: Kata (⤡), Rituals (☗), Shūji (Ⓒ)

Disarming Demeanor (School Ability): When you perform a Support action check, you may spend ☗ as follows:

☗+: Choose one character in the scene with vigilance lower than your school ranks plus ☗ spent this way. You treat that character's focus and vigilance each as 1 lower until the end of the scene.

Starting Outfit: Ceremonial clothes, traveling clothes, wakizashi (short sword) or scimitar, knife, journal, calligraphy set, Ide traveling pony (see page 141).

### Iuchi Horse Lord Disciple [Shugenja, Artisan]

The Iuchi's Horse Lord Disciples are followers of an ancient tradition, one established by the eldest child of Iuchi himself. As they are Rokugan's foremost experts in equine medicine, their services are highly sought throughout the Empire. Yet few know that they are specialized Meishōdō Masters, invoking equestrian spirits like ki-rin and the Fortune of Horses. Their unique meishōdō talismans are meant to adorn not samurai but their mounts, empowering horses with special abilities and spiritual power.

The Horse Lord's Favor (School Ability): When you make a check to craft, improve, or maintain an item, you may imprint it with the Horse Lord's Favor, giving it one of your advantages or disadvantages. If the item is a meishōdō talisman, you may instead give the item an advantage or disadvantage from its sealed spirit. While an item with your Horse Lord's Favor adorns a horse or a creature with the Loyal Steed ability, the horse or creature is Otherworldly and possesses the chosen advantage or disadvantage. If the item is a meishōdō talisman, the horse or creature may perform any techniques it contains. You can sustain a number of items with your Horse Lord's Favor up to your school rank.

Additionally, when you perform a Medicine check to remove fatigue or a condition from a horse or other equine creature, add bonus successes equal to your school rank.

Starting Outfit: Sanctified robes, traveling clothes, yumi (bow), quiver of arrows, wakizashi (short sword), two meishōdō talismans each containing a Rank 1 invocation, traveling pack, Iuchi riding steed (see page 141).

> **HORSE LORD INVOCATIONS**
>
> Like members of their sibling school, the Iuchi Meishōdō Masters, Iuchi Horse Lord Disciples know invocations by their foreign names.

### Kitsune Mediator School [Courtier, Sage]

The Fox Clan's lands are highly spiritually active; as a result, many of its members deal with spirits and supernatural beings far more often than the average samurai does. While the clan has a shugenja tradition, when one lives deep within a primordial forest, one must sometimes parley with spirits and other strange creatures just to survive. Having learned about such negotiations for generations, some members of the Fox Clan made it their personal mission to mediate in spiritual conflicts both within the Kitsune Forest and across Rokugan at large. Eventually, those efforts became more formalized with the foundation of the Kitsune Mediator School.

The Kitsune Mediators are often sent to places in Rokugan experiencing supernatural strife. While their assignments from the Fox Clan Champion are often to mediate between aggrieved human parties, their true goal is to determine if the problem is spiritual in nature and, if so, to help the human and supernatural inhabitants of the region reach a new accord that accommodates both. Though they are not fully initiated into the mysteries of the Fox Clan's shugenja tradition, Kitsune Mediators frequently work alongside them, helping to ease tensions on the human side and even undertaking rituals to appease angered spirits more directly when necessary. In recent days, however, the Kitsune Mediators have found themselves overwhelmed with disharmonies to pursue across Rokugan…

- Rituals: Commune with the Spirits
- Shūji (choose one): ❖ Appreciate the Scenery, Shallow Waters
Calm Counsel (School Ability): After a friendly character at range 0–4 receives strife, you may impose the Centered condition on that character (see page 133). You can use this school ability a number of times per scene up to your school rank.

Starting Outfit: Sanctified robes, wakizashi (short sword), knife, yumi (bow), quiver of arrows, calligraphy set, scroll satchel, traveling pack, bottle of sake, sweets (four servings).

### Kitsune Naturalist School [Shugenja]

Driven from its lands centuries ago, the Fox Clan found refuge in a vast and ancient wooded realm: the Kitsune Forest. The depths of this ancient woodland are rarely seen by humans, and nature spirits flourish here. It is even said that some of these spirits joined the clan, teaching the Kitsune arts for communing with nature spirits unknown to most humans. The shugenja of the Kitsune family learn to be especially attuned to the spirits of animals, plants, and other primordial forces in the world around them. Their tradition leads them to seek a balanced approach to their art, invoking the elements in equal measure rather than creating imbalances in their environment by calling upon any one element too heavily. While many trained in this tradition focus their time on studying how the Mortal Realm and its spiritual inhabitants interact in the Kitsune Forest, others are sent out into Rokugan alongside Kitsune Mediators to help ease tensions between humans and spirits when they occur.

Rings: +1 Air, +1 Earth

Starting Skills (choose three): +1 Courtesy, +1 Fitness, +1 Meditation, +1 Sentiment, +1 Survival, +1 Theology

Honor: 40

Techniques Available: Invocations (icon), Rituals (icon), Shūji (icon)

Natural Balance (School Ability): When you succeed on a check to activate an invocation, if it is the first invocation of that element you have activated this scene, you may add a number of bonus successes or kept dice set to [explosive-success symbol] equal to your school rank.

> **KITSUNE NATURALIST INVOCATIONS**
>
> The Kitsune Naturalists are concerned with the balance of nature and spend their time surrounded by the deep woods. Their tradition uses Elemental names for invocations. (See invocation Names and Traditions on page 190 in the core rulebook).

### Sand Road Wayfinder Tradition [Bushi, Shinobi]

The Sand Road is a dangerous place that spans a vast distance, sweeping across numerous regions and biomes. From the foothills of the Pillar of the Sky mountains to the sands around the Hidden Valley, each region has unique threats based on its terrain. As such, while the Unicorn Clan has worked hard to build trade routes across these areas, any trip can still have its perils, and there is no substitute for a regional guide. Most of the Sand Road's guides are locals who live along it, as they know best the perils of its terrain, its supernatural threats, and the humans who might be hostile to travelers. These guides use a variety of survival techniques, many of which depend on a guide's region and culture. This school can be used to represent a specialist from any of a number of regions that border the Sand Road.

Dunestrider (School Ability): At the start of each scene, choose one of the following terrain qualities: Dangerous, Entangling, Imbalanced, or Obscuring. Each friendly character within a number of range bands equal to your school rank ignores the negative effects of that terrain quality.

### Scholar of al-Zawira Tradition [Courtier]

In the city of al-Zawira, at the heart of the Cradle of the World, the Qamarist Caliphate's scholars push the bounds of knowledge in new and fascinating directions. Academics here ensconce themselves in deep studies of many fields, from human anatomy to philosophy, mathematics to alchemy, and theology to astronomy. Additionally, the massive scope of the Caliphate gives scholars access to knowledge from all corners of a vast domain. Between the libraries of the caliph and the archives of individual scholars, there is a near-unparalleled amount of knowledge in al-Zawira, at least within the Mortal Realm. This makes the scholars of the so-called City of Books especially sought after as researchers, advisors, and counselors in many courts, from the Ivory Kingdoms to the lands beyond the Great Northern Mountains.

Methodical Observation (School Ability): Once per scene when you make a Scholar skill check, you may reserve a number of dropped dice up to your school rank. Until the end of the scene, when you make a non-Scholar skill check, after choosing dice, you may add up to one of these reserved dice as a kept die.

### Shinjo Mounted Archer [Bushi, Artisan]

Ironically, one of the Great Clans’ most unconventional families champions one of the Empire’s oldest traditions. The Shinjo school of horseback archery is perhaps the family’s most renowned, due in no small part to the inherent difficulty of shooting arrows from horseback. Seeking the unity of horse and rider, students must steer with only their legs, release the bow at the most stable moment of a horse’s gallop, perform ritualized festival archery—such as yabusame and kasagake—and master fletching and bowcraft. Horse archers can even assist in worship and the appeasement of spirits, and many wander the lands seeking to do so.

When you spend a Void to seize the moment during a check using a bow, you may negate ⟳ symbols up to your school rank. If you succeed, gain bonus successes equal to the number of ⟳ symbols you negated.

Additionally, while you are riding your steed, all of your Martial Arts [Ranged] and Performance skill checks have unskilled assistance.

Lacquered armor, ceremonial clothes, daishō (katana or scimitar, wakizashi), Shinjo horsebow, quiver of arrows with three special arrows (one armor-piercing, one flesh-cutter, and one hummingbulb; see page 236 of the core rulebook), knife, traveling pack, bowyer’s kit, Shinjo courser (see page 143) or Unicorn Warhorse.

> **Reputation Sentinel (Mastery Ability) - Rank 6**
>
> Reputation Sentinel (Mastery Ability): You may use the Guard action during intrigues. When you do, increase the TN of Scheme action checks against the guarded target by 1, plus an additional 1 per two bonus successes.

### Utaku Silent Shadow Yōjimbō [Bushi, Courtier]

Assimilating back into the Empire—without sacrificing their identity—is perhaps the greatest diplomatic challenge the Unicorn have ever faced. A relatively young school, the Utaku Silent Shadow Yōjimbō School arose from the needs of this challenge, equipping its students not only to protect and advise the clan's diplomats but also to provide such services to those who otherwise cannot attain them. These stoic and calm individuals are known to speak few words, have unyielding convictions, and a lethal fighting prowess. Formed and maintained by the Utaku and Shinjo, this school marries both families' traditions and philosophies, protecting its charges in any terrain, deflecting both blade and damaging word.

Rings: +1 Air, +1 Earth

Starting Skills (Choose Five): +1 Courtesy, +1 Martial Arts [Melee], +1 Martial Arts [Ranged], +1 Meditation, +1 Sentiment, +1 Survival, +1 Tactics

Honor: 45

Techniques Available: Kata (✎), Rituals (⚚), Shūji (✒)

Warden of the Five Winds (School Ability): When you make a check to perform the Guard action, you gain a bonus success if you or your target is mounted on a steed. Additionally, when you succeed on a check to perform the Guard action, you may spend ☯ as follows: ☯+: Give a target at range 0–3 the Emboldened condition (see page 133). You can spend a maximum number of ☯ this way up to your school rank.

Starting Outfit: Lacquered armor, traveling clothes, daishō (katana or scimitar, wakizashi [short sword]), yari (spear), daikyū (bow), quiver of arrows, knife, traveling pack, Unicorn warhorse (see page 326 of the core rulebook) or Shinjo courser (see page 143).

### Ujik Nomad Tradition [Bushi]

The Ujik of the Plains of Wind and Stone live as nomadic herders in social groups called ordu, traveling with their herds across a vast swath of the continent. Ujik riders are nearly unparalleled; indeed, the Unicorn Clan learned much about cavalry from the Ujik when the two groups first met centuries ago. As a result of the Ujik's influence, the Unicorn adopted technologies and tactics like stirrups and the feigned retreat that would make them the greatest riders in the Emerald Empire upon their return. However, a member of an Ujik ordu must be able to do much more than ride. Most Ujik have a wide array of skills needed for their way of living: martial skills to protect their animals from predators and raiders alike, interpersonal skills to navigate encounters with other groups upon the plains, knowledge of animal husbandry and weather patterns to know where to move their herds. Of course, traditions tend to vary by ordu—some of which are small family groups, others massive moving cities of interwoven alliances.

Rings: +1 Earth, +1 Water

Starting Skills (choose five): +1 Fitness, +1 Martial Arts [Ranged], +1 Martial Arts [Unarmed], +1 Medicine, +1 Performance, +1 Survival, +1 Tactics

Honor: 40

Techniques Available: Kata (⤢), Rituals (卒), Shūji (Ꮝ)

Starting Techniques:

- Shūji: Call to Ride
- Rituals (choose one): Protection of the Flock, Wayfinder's Instincts
Move as the Wind (School Ability): When you make a Movement action check or make a check to travel at speed in narrative time, if you succeed, you may suffer up to 2 fatigue to add that many bonus successes. After you remove fatigue at the end of a scene, you may remove additional fatigue up to your school rank.

Starting Outfit: Traveling clothes, scimitar or yari (spear), Shinjo horsebow, quiver of arrows, knife, tent (yurt), travel rations (eight days), Unicorn warhorse (see page 326 of the core rulebook)
